Problem
-------

vmbus_alloc_ring() still allocates both ring halves with one order-7
(512 KiB) alloc_pages() call. Under buddy fragmentation that fails with
-ENOMEM while plenty of virtual memory is free. On WSL2 the visible
symptom is userspace control-plane failures such as "accept4 failed 110:
Connection timed out" on vsock channels. A dynamically instantiated
sub-channel cannot open, and the channel is dead for the life of the
fragmentation.

The failure is not exotic. Any long-running guest that allocates and
frees large objects - build workloads, in-memory databases, swap-heavy
tiering - will fragment the buddy to the point where no order-7 block
remains while thousands of order-0 pages are free.

Why not just vzalloc()
----------------------

The obvious fix is to fall back to vmalloc when the high-order
allocation fails. That is what v1 did, and it is wrong for Confidential
Computing. Ring memory must be host-visible, and a CoCo guest's private
memory has to be explicitly decrypted before the host can read it.
set_memory_decrypted() operates on the direct map and therefore needs
physically contiguous pages. A vmalloc range is not direct-mapped, so
decrypting it is not possible. arm64 CCA and Intel TDX guests without a
paravisor would break.

Kameron Carr's vmbus_alloc_buffer() already solves this: it allocates
the buffer as a series of physically contiguous chunks, descending from
a high order to order 0 as needed, decrypts each chunk with
set_memory_decrypted() where the platform requires it, and vmap()s the
chunks into one virtually contiguous range. v1 proposed a second
allocator. v2 removes the second allocator and routes everything through
the first.

What changed
------------

- vmbus_alloc_ring() and vmbus_free_ring() now use vmbus_alloc_buffer().
  The three related fields - buffer address, page array, and GPADL state
  - are grouped into struct vmbus_buffer, and struct vmbus_gpadl is
  embedded in it as buffer->gpadl so ownership cannot be split across
  two lifetimes. The struct vmbus_gpadl type itself is kept, because
  the previously exported vmbus_establish_gpadl(),
  vmbus_establish_gpadl_caller_decrypted() and vmbus_teardown_gpadl()
  still take a struct vmbus_gpadl *; the in-tree callers use the
  descriptor-aware _owned entry points instead.

- The generic VMBus UIO driver allocates its send and receive buffers
  the same way. Page and GPADL ownership travel together, which closes
  the window where a UIO mmap holds pages whose GPADL is still being
  established.

- Size rounding is validated before it is stored, and a cleanup with an
  uncertain outcome preserves the allocated chunks instead of freeing
  them. A buffer whose GPADL or encryption state cannot be proven is
  marked leaked and retained rather than handed back to the allocator
  while the host may still map it.

- GPADL message posting goes through a private callback so KUnit can
  inject header, body, and teardown post failures without a live host.
  The same helpers run in production, so the tested path is the shipped
  path.

- The order-descent loop is factored so KUnit can fail every order above
  zero and still exercise a real order-zero allocation, plus clean
  exhaustion when order zero also fails.

- The previously exported allocator, free, GPADL establish, caller-
  decrypted establish and teardown signatures are preserved in the
  merged tree, so external consumers of those symbols are unaffected.
  There is one intermediate shape worth naming: 1/9 gives
  vmbus_alloc_buffer() a bool confidential argument. The mainline
  function decides host-visibility from channel->co_external_memory
  alone, while a ring buffer on the same channel is governed by
  channel->co_ring_buffer, so the caller has to say which policy
  applies to the buffer it is allocating. 6/9 folds that argument
  into vmbus_alloc_buffer_owned() and restores vmbus_alloc_buffer()
  to the exact four-argument prototype mainline has today.
  vmbus_alloc_buffer_owned(), vmbus_establish_gpadl_owned(),
  vmbus_teardown_gpadl_owned() and vmbus_release_buffer() are new
  EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL symbols and are what the in-tree migrated callers
  use; they have to be exported because hv_netvsc and uio_hv_generic
  are separate modules from hv_vmbus. vmbus_onmessage() gains a
  host_generated argument so the message path can tell host
  revocation from local unload; it is not an exported symbol.

- The per-channel requestor array is allocated with kvcalloc() instead
  of kcalloc(). Its size follows the ring size, so at the default
  ring_size of 128 pages the array is larger than 256 KiB and kcalloc()
  would request an order-7 compound page -- the order-7 allocation on
  the channel-open path, and one of the two that fail with
  "sub channel open failed: -12" under buddy fragmentation. The array
  is guest-private request bookkeeping and never reaches the host, so
  vmalloc backing has no Confidential Computing implication.

- struct rndis_request is larger than KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE because it
  embeds two RNDIS_EXT_LEN (4 KiB) protocol tails, so kzalloc_obj()
  hands back an order-2 compound page on every RNDIS control request.
  That runs on the subchannel open path (netvsc_subchan_work ->
  get_rndis_request) and is the second high-order allocation there.
  Those tails are protocol capacity, not padding -- a response is
  memcpy'd into &request->response_msg and may spill into response_ext
  -- so the descriptor is not shrunk. It is allocated with
  kvzalloc_obj() instead: that keeps the zeroing kzalloc_obj()
  provided -- response_ext and request_ext are read after a response
  arrives, so an uninitialised tail would be an information leak --
  and the object is a control-path descriptor copied into the
  VMBus ring and is never DMA-mapped from this allocation, so vmalloc
  backing has no Confidential Computing implication, and kvmalloc()
  tries the kmalloc path with __GFP_NORETRY|__GFP_NOWARN first so it
  neither fails hard nor reaches the OOM killer when no compound page
  is available. Freed with kvfree() in the matching put_rndis_request().

- The shared-page decision is an explicit helper. Mainline took the
  chunked decrypt path from hv_is_isolation_supported() alone, so an
  Arm CCA or Intel TDX guest without a Hyper-V isolation property
  fell through to vzalloc() and could not re-encrypt its ring.
  Guest-memory encryption is reported through
  CC_ATTR_GUEST_MEM_ENCRYPT, independently of Hyper-V isolation, so
  vmbus_needs_shared_pages() consults both plus the per-buffer
  confidential flag. Ordinary private guests still take vzalloc().

- The reclaim workqueue teardown now cancels every timer-pending
  delayed_work before destroy_workqueue(). drain_workqueue() only
  drains work that is queued or running; a delayed_work still waiting
  on its timer lives in the timer wheel, not on the workqueue, so
  freeing the workqueue under an armed timer is a use-after-free.
  Owners are split into an internal free path (used by the reclaim
  work, which cannot wait for its own completion) and an external
  remove path that cancels the timer first. The shutdown walk cancels
  with the non-_sync variant under the owners lock, because reclaim
  work that is already running blocks on that same lock and
  destroy_workqueue() waits for it afterwards.

- HV_GPADL_BUFFER_DECRYPTED is gone. Once every caller goes through
  vmbus_alloc_buffer(), the distinction it carried is redundant.

- A completion that resolves to a NULL request address is no longer
  dereferenced. netvsc_send_pkt() registers (ulong)skb as the VMBus
  request address, and control RNDIS sends carry no skb -- rndis_filter
  calls netvsc_send() with skb == NULL -- so the registered address is
  NULL. That is intentional: there is no guest object to hand back, and
  the request slot is still allocated so the completion can reclaim it.
  netvsc_send_tx_complete() already tolerated it with if (likely(skb)).
  The empty-payload branch of netvsc_send_completion() did not: it cast
  the address to struct nvsp_message * and read hdr.msg_type
  unconditionally, which is a fatal NULL dereference in NAPI/softirq.
  This is not a ring-allocation change. It is reachable from this
  series because channel open now survives buddy fragmentation and
  control RNDIS traffic proceeds where it used to fail with -ENOMEM.
  It is kept in the series because the runtime drill has to be green on
  the bytes being posted; a trivial split is available if the netvsc
  maintainers prefer it standalone.

Mapping to the 2026-09-22 review
--------------------------------

  1. "Use vmbus_alloc_buffer() for rings, not only as a fallback."
     vmbus_alloc_ring() and vmbus_free_ring() are converted in 0001 and
     no second ring allocator remains beside them.

  2. "Group the buffer address, page array and GPADL state."
     struct vmbus_buffer in include/linux/hyperv.h, 0001.

  3. "Fold struct vmbus_gpadl into struct vmbus_buffer."
     channel->ringbuffer_gpadlhandle and channel->ringbuffer_page are
     replaced by channel->ringbuffer, whose GPADL is embedded as
     buffer->gpadl and is released by vmbus_release_buffer() as one
     unit, 0001 and 0006. The struct vmbus_gpadl type survives as the
     argument of the three previously exported establish/teardown
     prototypes; nothing that used to allocate it separately still
     does.

  4. "A leak flag when GPADL or CoCo re-encryption cannot be proven."
     gpadl.leak and buffer.leak are set when a GPADL message may have
     reached the host but completion is uncertain, so the backing pages
     are retained instead of handed back, 0001 and 0006.

  5. "Eliminate redundant HV_GPADL_* types once vmbus_alloc_buffer() is
     universal." HV_GPADL_BUFFER_DECRYPTED is gone. The caller-decrypted
     case is a memory_prepared argument to the establish path rather
     than a third GPADL type, 0001.

