  What does repeat is the claim the series is about: channel open
  survives. On all twenty-two guest-runs the hv_netvsc rebind comes
  back (nic_rebound=yes), with zero accept4 failures and zero oops,
  at every measured depth -- including the three that stopped with a
  single order-7 block still free. Where depletion was reached the
  verdict is PASS_RING_ALLOCATION_UNDER_FRAGMENTATION, scoped as
  ordinary-x86_64-vzalloc-path; co-co-chunked-fallback-not-exercised.

  On an ordinary x86_64 guest vmbus_alloc_buffer() always takes
  vzalloc(), so the chunked order-N -> order-0 degrade is not what ran
  here; that path is covered by fault injection and stays open on CoCo
  hardware.

  The drill's post-ready path is fork-free. After ready=1 every parse,
  every buddy and map count, and the channel rebind run on shell
  builtins until the helper is torn down. That constraint is
  load-bearing regardless of the numbers a given run reports: a munmap
  of 16 KiB frees four order-0 pages into the per-cpu page cache rather
  than one order-2 buddy block, drain_all_pages() never runs against a
  fully mlocked pattern, and earlier runs lost the shell and the
  console to a clone at exactly that moment. So the helper's
  groups=504 pages=2016 are a munmap tally, not forkable stacks, and
  buddy_order2= is reported from the buddy's own order-2-and-above
  count for information -- 0, 4, 5, 286, 305, 333, 339, 342, 362,
  and 466 have all been seen depending on the run, and it is not
  what the post-ready path trusts.

  A green drill job is not by itself a gate closure -- the gate is the
  RESULT, VERDICT and MMAP_HOLD lines in the guest console.

  An earlier run against a build that still carried a second high-order
  allocation on the subchannel-open path -- the RNDIS request
