  same negative on the same bytes with one guest short of depletion,
  and 36978224340 repeats it on the revised commit messages. Both
  runs report order7_dmesg=0 accept4_failures=0 oops=0 rebind=yes
  and LIFECYCLE_VERDICT=PASS cycles=100 phase2=yes on every guest.
  The crash was in the completion path for a control RNDIS send, and
  those sends are part of every one of the 100 rebind cycles, so
  this is the surface that failed in 36960219285, shown surviving.

What is not proven here
-----------------------

CoCo page-state transitions. SEV-SNP, TDX, and Arm CCA memory-state
behaviour has not been observed on real confidential hardware. The
per-chunk decrypt design is what those platforms need, and the static
invariants are machine-checked in CI, but that is a negative proof about
this patch set, not platform evidence. Platform validation is follow-up
work and this series should be read with that gap open.

Note also that the chunked order-N -> order-0 degrade only runs when
vmbus_needs_shared_pages() is true: a non-confidential buffer on a
guest where hv_is_isolation_supported() or
cc_platform_has(CC_ATTR_GUEST_MEM_ENCRYPT) holds. An ordinary x86_64
guest always takes the single vzalloc() path inside
vmbus_alloc_buffer(), so the runtime numbers above speak to ring
lifecycle and fragmentation resilience, not to the confidential
fallback. That fallback is covered by the KUnit injection tests and
awaits platform validation.

The design follows Michael Kelley's review of the v1 series. Comments
welcome.

